    "title": "Cars in Cities",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Many big cities have a serious problem with traffic. Cars fill the streets, the air is dirty, and people spend a lot of time waiting in queues. Some people think we should stop cars in city centres. Others disagree."
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "People who want fewer cars give three main reasons. First, cars make the air dirty, and this causes health problems like asthma. Second, there are many accidents with cars, especially near schools. Third, cars use a lot of space. A car park for one hundred cars could be a park for children instead."
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "However, other people say that cars are necessary. Shop owners worry that customers will not come if they cannot park. Older people and people with disabilities often find buses and trains difficult to use. Also, in some places the buses are slow, crowded and expensive, so people prefer their own car."
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Some cities have found a middle way. In Oslo, cars are not allowed in the centre, but the city has built many new bike roads and made the buses cheaper. In London, drivers pay a fee to enter the centre, and the money is used for public transport. These changes work best when there are good alternatives to the car."
      }
    ]
  },
